// Koru Standard Library: the `emit` part of json.kz — schema-directed
// compile-time JSON emission as control flow. Joined via `~part emit` in
// json.kz; merged into the json module at load.
//
// `std/json:emit` is the write-side twin of `std/json:parse`: the record
// literal at the `value:` position IS the output schema — keys are the JSON
// key names, and each value expression's type is resolved at compile time
// from the bindings in scope at the site:
//
//   std/json:emit(value: { service: service, port: port, tls: tls })
//   | ok body |> send(body) |> std/json:emit.free(text: body)
//
// A bare identifier `emit(value: cfg)` emits every field of a branch-payload
// record bound upstream (`| ok cfg` on a tor whose payload declares
// named fields).
//
// Value grammar: a binding name resolved through scopeAtSite (destructure
// fields, `| branch name` payload binds, `: name` return binds, impl input
// fields), a `binding.field` path into a record payload, a nested `{...}`
// record literal, or a string/number/bool/null literal. Supported field
// types: `string` (escaped at the splice), `bool`, the int types, the float
// types (non-finite emits `null` — JSON cannot spell inf/nan), `?T` (null
// or inner), `[]T`. A bare destructure leaf has no type — it cannot be
// resolved and is refused with a teaching error.
//
// Ownership: `ok` mints `string<emitted!>`, discharged ONLY by
// `std/json:emit.free` — the same contract as yyjson's `render`/`render.free`,
// one allocation, koru_allocator-owned, no arena. The output is independent
// of every input binding's lifetime.
//
// The transform emits no DOM and no builder tree: a per-site escaping
// writer, a number writer, and an inline byte-append sequence specialized
// to the resolved schema.

// The tor decl declares the USER surface: `emit(...): body` binds a string
// carrying <emitted!>, discharged by `emit.free`. The transform proc returns
// SiteResult internally — the decl's return is what the call site produces.
~[comptime|transform]pub tor emit { value: string, reporter: *std/compiler:ErrorReporter } -> string<emitted!>

// Discharge the `<emitted!>` obligation — the emitted text is owned by
// koru_allocator(), so free is the matching call. Same contract as
// yyjson's render.free.
~pub tor emit.free { text: string<!emitted> }

~proc emit.free|zig {
    koru_allocator().free(text);
}
~[transform]proc emit|zig {
    return .{ .transformed = EmitDrive.drive(allocator, reporter, item, invocation, program, .heap) };
}
